issue_context.body:
### Background and motivation

When logging the time passed in a Stopwatch, some may write `Stopwatch.ToString()`. This is a mistake as it always returns the string `System.Diagnostics.Stopwatch`. 

We should make it return a reasonable default representation of the elapsed time. That would satisfy the principle of least surprise, as well as be a little more compact when writing a little test program and dumping the time. 

### API Proposal

```csharp
namespace System.Diagnostics;

public partial class Stopwatch
{
    public override string ToString() => Elapsed.ToString();
}
```


### API Usage

The scenario here is probably a simple test app, since otherwise you'd care more about formatting.

```csharp
var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
// do something 
Console.WriteLine(sw.ToString());
```


### Alternative Designs

It could choose a different format, but `TimeSpan.ToString()` (which uses format 'c') is already the default on TimeSpan and consistent across locales. Also, it's what's used in the DebuggerDisplay, which is `$"{Elapsed} (IsRunning = {_isRunning})"`.

### Risks

Someone depending on ToString() returning the hard coded type name for some diagnostic purpose. Without hard data that that is common, this seems an acceptable risk.

